`,s.jsxs(a.p,{children:["In this discussion, we presented a logistic construction of the real numbers and their properties within the formal language of set theory starting from first principles, using only the axioms and results of first-order logic and a set of axioms for set theory, along with the axiom of choice. Within this rather limited set of axioms, we managed to create a solid foundation for math. The reader can trivially extend ",s.jsxs(a.span,{className:"katex",children:[s.jsx(a.span,{className:"katex-mathml",children:s.jsx(a.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:s.jsxs(a.semantics,{children:[s.jsx(a.mrow,{children:s.jsx(a.mi,{mathvariant:"double-struck",children:"R"})}),s.jsx(a.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\mathbb{R}"})]})})}),s.jsx(a.span,{className:"katex-html","aria-hidden":"true",children:s.jsxs(a.span,{className:"base",children:[s.jsx(a.span,{className:"strut",style:{height:"0.6889em"}}),s.jsx(a.span,{className:"mord mathbb",children:"R"})]})})]})," to complex number ",s.jsxs(a.span,{className:"katex",children:[s.jsx(a.span,{className:"katex-mathml",children:s.jsx(a.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:s.jsxs(a.semantics,{children:[s.jsx(a.mrow,{children:s.jsx(a.mi,{mathvariant:"double-struck",children:"C"})}),s.jsx(a.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\mathbb{C}"})]})})}),s.jsx(a.span,{className:"katex-html","aria-hidden":"true",children:s.jsxs(a.span,{className:"base",children:[s.jsx(a.span,{className:"strut",style:{height:"0.6889em"}}),s.jsx(a.span,{className:"mord mathbb",children:"C"})]})})]})," by simply taking ordered pairs of reals. Thus it is possible to construct most of analysis inside set theory, and indeed most of mathematics. It should surprise the reader that there are still two ZF axioms we have not presented, since they were not necessary for our discussion. These are the ",s.jsx(a.em,{children:"axiom of regularity"})," and the ",s.jsx(a.em,{children:"axiom schema of replacement"}),", both of which are necessary for dealing with infinite sets and ordinals, the former of which we barely started discussing and the latter we haven't even properly mentioned."]}),`
